# EXPECT: ends its own top level with a `return`
#
# THE POINT OF THIS FIXTURE is that `return` at a file's top level means THREE
# different things depending on which road the file arrived by, and the third
# road was the one left unguarded:
#
#   main program      end the program, exit 0, value discarded
#   imported module   end the module; bindings below it never exist
#   load_file target  end THAT FILE only, yield its value to the call, and the
#                     CALLER CONTINUES
#
# Splicing collapses all three into "end the program":
#
#   VM   main-start / inc-start / main-end      AOT   main-start / inc-start
#
# This predates the #146 emission change — but that change made it QUIETER, not
# louder. The old code emitted `return 7` from main, so the binary exited 7
# against the VM's 0 and the suite's exit-code contract caught it. Emitting
# exit-0 left stdout as the only difference, which is precisely the silent class
# #146 existed to remove. A fix that improves one road can degrade another to
# below the gate's resolution.
#
# Measured cost of refusing: 0 of the 55 literal load_file targets across the
# ecosystem (371 references).
#
# The over-firing side is already gated by t76_load_file_paths, whose target
# has two `return`s inside `define` bodies and must keep splicing.

print of "main-start"
load_file of "_lfret.eigs"
print of "main-end"
